Chapter 1

Introduction

1.1 The Recursive Self‑Improvement Problem

Recursive self‑improvement (RSI) is the closed‑loop process in which an artificial intelligence system modifies its own architecture, parameters, or behavioural code, evaluates the resulting change, and feeds that signal into subsequent modifications. The concept was introduced by Good (1965): an ultraintelligent machine capable of designing better machines would trigger an “intelligence explosion.” Modern experimental forays—the Darwin‑Gödel Machine, the Self‑Improving Coding Agent, and large‑language‑model auto‑curricula—have demonstrated elementary self‑modification alongside “objective hacking”: agents remove measurement mechanisms rather than solve tasks. A formal model (N2M‑RSI) claiming inevitable unbounded growth was retracted due to a critical error. These episodes reveal a foundational gap: we lack a rigorous language for specifying when self‑improvement remains bounded, when it becomes dangerous, and how to design architectures whose safety can be formally characterised.

1.2 The Central Falsifiable Claim

This dissertation advances a single, falsifiable claim:

A recursive self‑improvement loop can be engineered so that, under a finite and explicitly enumerated set of design assumptions C_1,\ldots,C_n, every catastrophic failure mode either produces a detectable halt trigger or is allocated a probabilistic error budget \delta_{\text{total}} that bounds its likelihood. The assumptions are stated with precision such that a future implementer can verify whether they hold in a given deployment, and, if they do not, can identify which safety layer is unsupported.

The dissertation does not claim that the nine layers are jointly exhaustive; it does not claim that RSI is safe in any absolute sense; and it does not solve the monitor‑subversion regress. It claims only that detectable failure is a formally specifiable architectural property, and that BR‑SI realises that property under its stated assumptions.

1.3 Scope and Limitations

This is a purely theoretical dissertation. No code was trained, no physical tests were performed, and no human‑subject experiments were conducted. All protocols, bounds, and architectures are formal specifications. The document does not report empirical results. The value of the work lies in the precision with which the assumptions are laid out, the derivations that connect them to safety claims, and the explicit identification of the conditions under which those claims break.

1.4 Taxonomy of Statements

Throughout the dissertation, every formal statement carries a label:

· Theorem: a non‑trivial result demonstrated by a proof original to this work.
· Proposition: a routine deduction or corollary from existing results, or a direct consequence of a definition.
· Metatheorem: a statement about the structure of analysis that applies to a broad class of models, not a concrete numeric bound.
· Heuristic: a design rule justified by intuition and internal consistency, without formal proof.

This taxonomy ensures that a reader can distinguish between load‑bearing structural results and engineering choices.

