Chapter 2

Related Work: Why Existing Methods Do Not Address RSI Safety

Before constructing BR‑SI, we must demonstrate that established approaches to AI safety and formal verification are individually insufficient for the RSI setting. A complete defence of a new architecture requires this negative claim.

2.1 Verified Neural Networks and Formal Methods

Abstraction‑based verification (AI², Reluplex, DeepPoly) can prove that the output of a fixed neural network remains within prescribed bounds for a given input set. These methods, however, assume a static network and a static specification. In an RSI loop, the network architecture and parameters change under the agent’s own control; verification must be re‑run after every self‑modification. The verifier itself then becomes part of the system’s attack surface: a sufficiently capable self‑improver could learn to produce modifications that are verifier‑passing but semantically degraded. Moreover, verification addresses only the current behaviour, not the downstream effects of a modification on future learning dynamics. Runtime shielding similarly enforces a property on a pre‑existing policy without addressing the fact that the policy itself is being rewritten.

2.2 Conformal Prediction and Distribution‑Free Guarantees

Conformal prediction provides finite‑sample coverage sets with no distributional assumptions, under the hypothesis of exchangeability. In an RSI loop, data are self‑generated and the model drifts over time; exchangeability is violated from the very first self‑modification. Adaptations of conformal inference to non‑exchangeable settings (e.g., adaptive conformity scores) degrade coverage bounds in proportion to the cumulative distribution shift, which is precisely the quantity the RSI process controls. Thus, conformal prediction can form a component of a monitor but cannot by itself guarantee safety when the shift is adversarial.

2.3 PAC‑Bayes and Statistical Learning Theory

Generalisation bounds from PAC‑Bayes or uniform convergence assume a fixed hypothesis class. Self‑improvement expands the effective hypothesis class over time, violating this assumption. Furthermore, standard bounds provide guarantees on the data‑generating distribution, not under counterfactual interventions. A self‑improving system that learns to manipulate its proxy metric will appear to improve within the observational distribution while degrading under interventions—a failure mode that standard learning theory cannot detect.

2.4 The Original Gödel Machine and the Statistical Gödel Machine

The Gödel Machine requires a formal proof of expected‑utility increase; this is unavailable in high‑dimensional machine learning. The Statistical Gödel Machine (SGM) replaces proofs with anytime‑valid statistical tests. The SGM provides a foundation for the acceptance gate in BR‑SI, but it assumes exchangeable evaluation data and does not address the monitor‑subversion regress. The present work extends the SGM to non‑i.i.d. settings, embeds it within a multi‑layer safety stack, and introduces a watchtower to partially address the meta‑safety problem.

2.5 Positioning BR‑SI

No existing single approach addresses the conjunction of (a) self‑modification, (b) adversarial self‑awareness, and (c) evaluation circularity that defines the RSI safety problem. BR‑SI is a compositional architecture: it layers multiple partial guarantees, each addressing a distinct failure mode, and adds a meta‑layer to monitor the monitors. The compositional analysis (Chapter 11) demonstrates that the ensemble does not create silent blind spots. This positioning also underscores the Honesty Principle: since no single method provides global causal certification, the framework instead renders failures detectable.

