Chapter 3

State Space and the Self‑Improvement Operator

We now define the mathematical objects that describe a recursive self‑improvement system. All subsequent safety layers operate on these objects.

3.1 System State

Definition 3.1 (System State).
At discrete time t \in \mathbb{N}, the state of the RSI system is a triple

S_t = (\theta_t, M_t, D_t),
\]  

where

· \theta_t \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d are the model parameters (weights, biases), with d \gg 1;
· M_t \in \mathcal{M} is an internal world model, which can be any predictive map from observations and actions to a latent representation;
· D_t \in \mathcal{D} is an experience buffer containing trajectories (x,a,r,x') and self‑generated verification traces.

The state space \mathcal{S} = \Theta \times \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{D} is a product space. For theoretical analysis we assume that \Theta and \mathcal{M} are measurable spaces; the details of the representation do not affect the arguments.

Definition 3.2 (Capability Functional).
A capability functional C: \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}_+ quantifies performance. A concrete choice is C(S_t) = -\mathbb{E}_{x \sim D_{\text{shift}}}[L(\theta_t, x)], the negative expected loss on a shifted test distribution, but the framework is independent of the specific C.

3.2 Stochastic Self‑Improvement Operator

Because improvement attempts are noisy (gradient estimation, non‑deterministic generation, random search), the transition to the next state is stochastic.

Definition 3.3 (Stochastic Self‑Improvement Operator).
Let \mathbb{B} \subset \mathbb{R}_+ be the set of admissible compute budgets. A self‑improvement operator is a measurable map

\mathcal{U} : \mathcal{S} \times \mathbb{B} \to \Delta(\mathcal{S}),
\]  

where \Delta(\mathcal{S}) is the space of probability measures over \mathcal{S}. The output S_{t+1} \sim \mathcal{U}(S_t, B) is drawn according to the distribution induced by the operator.

3.3 GVU Decomposition

It is useful to decompose \mathcal{U} into distinct phases: generation of candidate modifications, evaluation, update, and gating. This is the Generate‑Verify‑Update (GVU) schema.

Definition 3.4 (GVU Decomposition).
The operator \mathcal{U} can be factored as

\mathcal{U} = \mathcal{U}_{\text{gate}} \circ \mathcal{U}_{\text{update}} \circ \mathcal{U}_{\text{eval}} \circ \mathcal{U}_{\text{gen}},
\]  

where

· \mathcal{U}_{\text{gen}} : \mathcal{S} \times \mathbb{B} \to \Delta(\mathcal{C}) produces a distribution over candidate modifications \mathcal{C};
· \mathcal{U}_{\text{eval}} : \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}^m evaluates a candidate on m metrics;
· \mathcal{U}_{\text{update}} : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{C}^* \to \mathcal{S} applies an accepted candidate;
· \mathcal{U}_{\text{gate}} : \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{H}_t \to \{0,1\} is the statistical acceptance gate, where \mathcal{H}_t is the history up to time t.

This decomposition isolates the safety decision (gate) from the generation process, enabling the independent analysis of each phase.

3.4 Intervention Probes

To test causal robustness—not merely distributional generalisation—we require a set of counterfactual interventions that the system must withstand.

Definition 3.5 (Intervention Probe Set \mathcal{A}_0).
Let \mathcal{X} be the input space. An intervention a: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X} is a deterministic transformation instantiating the do(A=a) operator of Pearl’s causal calculus. \mathcal{A}_0 is a finite set of such transformations, chosen from a domain‑appropriate causal ontology (e.g., entity swaps, tense shifts, API substitutions). \mathcal{A}_0 is fixed before any self‑improvement cycle and is never modified by the system.

The finiteness of \mathcal{A}_0 is critical; it implies that the safety filter cannot certify global causal robustness, a point we formalise in Chapter 4.

