Chapter 6

Delay‑Differential Stability of Recursive Improvement

Evaluation of a candidate modification takes time, and this delay feeds back into the improvement loop. For systems becoming more capable, evaluation often requires more compute or longer simulations, making the delay state‑dependent. This chapter models the resulting dynamics and extracts structural constraints.

6.1 State‑Dependent Delay‑Differential Equation Model

Definition 6.1 (SD‑DDE Model Schema).
Let C(t): \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+ denote the system’s capability at time t, E(t) the applied compute, and I/O(t) summarise information throughput. The evaluation delay depends on the current capability: \tau = \tau(C), with \tau'(C) \ge 0. The dynamics are governed by a functional differential equation with state‑dependent delay:

\frac{dC}{dt} = f\bigl(C(t-\tau(C(t))),\, E(t),\, I/O(t)\bigr), \qquad (t \ge 0).
\]  

The function f: \mathbb{R}_+^3 \to \mathbb{R} is assumed to be continuously differentiable, with \partial f / \partial C < 0 (negative feedback from delayed capability) and bounded first derivatives.

Equation (6.1) captures the essential feedback structure: the rate of improvement depends on the past state of the system, evaluated at a delay that increases with the current state.

6.2 Linear Stability Analysis as a Metatheorem

Since f and \tau are not given a specific parametric form, we cannot obtain concrete stability thresholds. Instead, we demonstrate the form of the analysis.

Metatheorem 6.1 (Existence of a Characteristic Equation).
Assume there exists an equilibrium capability C^* such that f(C^*,E^*,I/O^*) = 0. Linearising (6.1) around this equilibrium yields, to first order in the deviation x(t) = C(t) - C^*,

\dot{x}(t) = -\alpha\, x(t-\tau(C^*)) - \beta\, x(t),
\]  

where \alpha = -(\partial f / \partial C_{\text{delayed}}) > 0, \beta = -(\partial f / \partial C_{\text{instantaneous}}) \ge 0. Solutions of the form x(t) = e^{\lambda t} lead to the transcendental characteristic equation

\lambda + \beta + \alpha e^{-\lambda \tau^*} = 0, \qquad \tau^* := \tau(C^*).
\]  

The linear stability of the equilibrium is determined by the location of the roots of (6.3): if all roots have strictly negative real parts, the equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable; if any root has a positive real part, it is unstable.

Proof.
The linearisation follows from a standard functional derivative for discrete‑delay equations, combined with the chain rule for the state‑dependent delay term, assuming \tau'(C) is absorbed into the effective coefficients after evaluating at equilibrium. For the sake of brevity, we omit the routine intermediate steps; the form (6.2) is standard in the DDE literature. ∎

Metatheorem 6.2 (Existence of a Critical Delay).
For the pure delayed‑feedback case \beta = 0, the equation \lambda + \alpha e^{-\lambda \tau^*} = 0 has purely imaginary roots \lambda = i\omega when \omega\tau^* = \pi/2 and \omega = \alpha. Thus, the minimal delay for which instability first appears is

\tau_c = \frac{\pi}{2\alpha}.
\]  

For \beta > 0, a unique critical delay \tau_c(\alpha,\beta) exists beyond which the equilibrium is unstable; it may be found numerically by locating the smallest \tau at which a root crosses the imaginary axis.

Sketch.
Setting \lambda = i\omega and separating real and imaginary parts gives \beta + \alpha\cos(\omega\tau^*) = 0 and \omega - \alpha\sin(\omega\tau^*) = 0. From these, one can deduce that a positive solution for \tau^* exists if and only if certain inequalities hold; the smallest such \tau^* is \tau_c. The existence of a stability switch is a classical result for equations with a single discrete delay. ∎

Metatheorem 6.3 (Shrinking Stability Margin with State‑Dependent Delay).
If \tau'(C) > 0, then the critical delay \tau_c(C^*) is a decreasing function of the equilibrium capability C^*. That is, higher capability implies a smaller safe delay margin.

Proof.
The coefficients \alpha and \beta in the linearisation are evaluated at C^* and may in general depend on C^*. More importantly, the actual delay at equilibrium is \tau(C^*). As C^* increases, \tau(C^*) grows by assumption, while \tau_c(C^*) typically varies more slowly or even decreases; the net effect is that the distance \tau_c(C^*) - \tau(C^*) narrows. The formal statement that \frac{d}{dC^*}(\tau_c - \tau) < 0 follows from differentiating the implicit stability condition with respect to C^* and employing \tau'(C) > 0. The details are omitted because the functional forms of f and \tau are unspecified; the proposition holds structurally for the class of models we consider. ∎

6.3 The Dwell‑Time Safety Rule (Structural Requirement)

The DDE literature demonstrates that near the stability boundary, systems can exhibit hysteresis, stability switches, and secondary attractors that are not evident from a point‑wise check. Consequently, verifying \tau(C(t)) < \tau_c at a single instant is insufficient.

Heuristic 6.1 (Dwell‑Time Requirement).
The RSI loop shall be considered in a safe operating regime only if

\tau(C(t)) < \tau_c(C(t)) - \Delta_{\text{hys}}
\]  

holds continuously over a time window of length T_{\text{dwell}}. The parameters \Delta_{\text{hys}} (hysteresis margin) and T_{\text{dwell}} (dwell time) are architecture‑dependent and must be calibrated via numerical bifurcation analysis of a concrete f and \tau when the system is deployed. In the absence of such calibration in this theoretical work, the rule is a structural requirement: any safe RSI loop must include such a dwell‑time check on evaluation delay.

The dwell‑time requirement becomes a component of the safety stack (Layer L3) and is monitored continuously by the meta‑safety watchtower.

