Chapter 7

Contamination Prevention: Dual‑Holdout and the Social Contamination Threat

A self‑improving system generates its own training data. Without rigorous isolation, evaluation data inevitably leak into the training corpus, causing benchmark inflation and “model autophagy.” This chapter specifies the dual‑holdout protocol and then confronts a deeper, often‑overlooked contamination pathway: indirect influence on human annotators.

7.1 Dual‑Holdout Protocol

Protocol 7.1 (Dual‑Holdout Design).

1. Monitoring set D_{\text{gt}}^{\text{mon}}: Periodically refreshed from fresh human annotation, conducted on air‑gapped workstations with no network connection to the RSI system. Used exclusively for drift detection.
2. Terminal audit set D_{\text{gt}}^{\text{term}}: Generated once, before any self‑improvement cycle, by human annotators on similarly isolated hardware. Its SHA‑256 hash is published; the plaintext content is never released to the RSI system. Used exactly once for final publication/regulatory audit, then cryptographically destroyed.

Drift detection: Every K improvement cycles, we compute the Kullback‑Leibler divergence between the model’s predictive distribution and the ground‑truth distribution on the monitoring set:

D_{\text{KL}}\bigl(\mathbb{P}_{S_t}(y|x) \,\|\, \mathbb{P}_{\text{gt}}(y|x)\bigr)_{x \sim D_{\text{gt}}^{\text{mon}}}.
\]  

If this divergence exceeds a pre‑defined threshold \tau_{\text{drift}} for three consecutive checks, the loop is halted.

Cross‑generational protection: The hash of D_{\text{gt}}^{\text{term}} is entered into a public registry; future training data pipelines filter against this registry. The cost of periodic human annotation is explicitly budgeted.

7.2 Social Contamination: Formalising the Threat

The standard contamination model assumes annotators are independent of the AI system. In a world where AI‑generated text, code, and media permeate the cultural environment, this assumption is questionable. Even air‑gapped annotators are members of society and may absorb AI‑influenced norms, vocabulary, and reasoning patterns. This is social contamination.

We model the phenomenon as follows. Let A_t be the distribution of human annotator judgments at time t, and G_t the distribution of AI‑generated content circulating in the cultural sphere. The annotator distribution evolves as

A_{t+1} = (1-\gamma) A_t + \gamma \, \Phi(G_t),
\]  

where \Phi is a “cultural absorption” operator and \gamma \in [0,1] is the social influence rate. The ground‑truth label set D_{\text{gt}}^{\text{mon}} is sampled from A_t. If G_t is correlated with the model’s outputs, then the “ground truth” drifts in the same direction as the model, making contamination undetectable by standard drift checks.

Conjecture 7.1 (Undetectability of Social Contamination under Global AI Deployment).
In a regime where AI‑generated content dominates public discourse (\gamma large) and the RSI system contributes significantly to G_t, the simultaneous drift of A_t and the model’s predictions can maintain the KL divergence in Protocol 7.1 below threshold even as the model’s alignment with the original human intent degrades. Under mild identifiability assumptions, no purely data‑based drift detector can distinguish this coupled drift from a stable aligned system.

Justification sketch.
The joint evolution of model and annotator forms a non‑linear dynamical system. When the model’s outputs shift G_t and G_t feeds back into A_t, the KL divergence between model and annotator can remain small if the coupling \Phi is strong, even as both move away from an original reference. The standard causal identification assumptions (no unobserved confounders, independent mechanisms) are violated because the system’s outputs confound the annotator distribution. ∎

Partial Mitigation (Diagnostic).
We propose a cross‑pod temporal correlation test. Annotators are organised into geographically and culturally distinct pods (e.g., different countries, professional backgrounds) with minimised cross‑contamination. If a synchronised shift in annotator judgments across all pods is observed that tracks the semantic trajectory of the model’s outputs (even with a time lag), a contamination alarm is raised. This test does not eliminate social contamination; it merely converts some silent contaminations into detectable events. The problem remains fundamentally open.

