Chapter 8

Goodhart’s Law: Tail Diagnostics and Utility Measurement

When a proxy metric M is optimised, true utility G can degrade catastrophically even while the correlation between G and M remains high, provided the discrepancy D = G-M is heavy‑tailed (Majka & El‑Mhamdi, 2025). This chapter operationalises the detection of such heavy‑tailed discrepancies and specifies an air‑gapped utility measurement protocol.

8.1 Tail Diagnostic with the Hill Estimator

Let \{D_i\}_{i=1}^n be a sample of discrepancy observations between a proxy F and a utility function u \in \mathcal{U}. Assume the distribution of D has a regularly varying right tail with tail index \xi = 1/\alpha. Heavy‑tailedness corresponds to \xi > 0.5 (infinite variance for discrepancies).

Definition 8.1 (Hill Estimator).
Let D_{(1)} \le \cdots \le D_{(n)} be the order statistics. For an integer k with k \to \infty and k/n \to 0 as n \to \infty, the Hill estimator of the tail index is

\hat{\xi}_k = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k} \log\frac{D_{(n-i+1)}}{D_{(n-k)}}.

The choice of k is critical; \hat{\xi}_k is sensitive to k in finite samples. We adopt a bootstrap stability criterion.

Algorithm 8.1 (Sensitivity‑Aware Hill Estimator).
Given a discrepancy sample of size n:

1. For each candidate k in a grid \{\lfloor n^{0.5}\rfloor, \dots, \lfloor n^{0.9}\rfloor\}, compute \hat{\xi}_k.
2. For each such k, compute a bootstrap estimate of the standard error \mathrm{SE}(\hat{\xi}_k) using B resamples.
3. Select the largest k for which \mathrm{SE}(\hat{\xi}_k) < \epsilon_{\text{boot}} (e.g., 0.1).
4. If no such k exists, declare the sample insufficient and pause the improvement loop.
5. Output the selected \hat{\xi}_k.

Safety Trigger L5 (Goodhart Heavy‑Tail).
If \hat{\xi}_k > 0.5 at any re‑audit, a heavy‑tail event is declared. The risk budget \epsilon_{\text{risk}} is halved and deployment is frozen until the source of the heavy‑tailed discrepancy is attributed and remedied.

8.2 Air‑Gapped Utility Measurement

The set of utility functions \mathcal{U} is a moving target and a potential attack surface. We specify the following protocol.

Protocol 8.1 (Blind, Air‑Gapped Utility Elicitation and Gate Decoupling).

· Blind elicitation: At each re‑audit, a fresh cohort of human annotators provides pairwise preferences on model outputs. The annotators see only the two options (A,B) with no model‑generated context; workstations are physically isolated from any network the RSI system can access.
· Gate decoupling: The deployment acceptance gate uses a frozen snapshot \mathcal{U}_{t-90} (a utility function from 90 days prior), not the freshly elicited \mathcal{U}_t. This prevents the RSI system from adapting to real‑time utility changes within a single deployment window.
· Robustness testing (future work): The previously proposed adversarial annotator injection is removed from this dissertation because of unresolved ethical design requirements; its exploration must occur under a separate, ethics‑board‑reviewed protocol.

The decoupling introduces a lag that trades off responsiveness to genuine value change against resistance to adversarial manipulation of the utility function. The 90‑day window is a placeholder; the optimal lag depends on the deployment domain.

