Chapter 9

Latency Poisoning as an Attack Vector

The external validator f_{\text{ext}} (e.g., a code executor, a physical simulator, a human evaluator) is a critical component of the evaluation phase \mathcal{U}_{\text{eval}}. Attackers can manipulate the validator’s timing without altering the correctness of its outputs, drawing on the “sponge poisoning” literature. This chapter formalises latency poisoning as a threat to the SD‑DDE dynamics.

9.1 Adversary Model

We define a concrete adversary.

Definition 9.1 (Latency Poisoning Adversary).
The adversary can submit up to N_{\text{adv}} specially crafted inputs per evaluation cycle to the validator queue. These inputs are designed to trigger worst‑case computational complexity (e.g., pathological programs for a code executor, adversarial physical configurations for a simulator), thereby increasing the validator’s average processing time by an amount \Delta\tau_{\text{adv}}. The adversary has no access to the validator’s internal state; they can observe only the timing of their own submissions and the overall system throughput. The adversary’s goal is to push the effective evaluation delay past the critical threshold \tau_c, destabilising the RSI loop.

9.2 Analytical Link to SD‑DDE Stability

Proposition 9.1 (Conditional Latency‑Induced Instability).
Assume the RSI dynamics are well described by the SD‑DDE model schema of Chapter 6, with an equilibrium capability C^* and critical delay \tau_c(C^*). If the adversary injects an additive latency such that

\tau_{\text{eff}} := \tau(C^*) + \Delta\tau_{\text{adv}} > \tau_c(C^*)
\]  

for a duration exceeding the characteristic relaxation time of the system, then the linearised dynamics become unstable and the capability trajectory diverges from C^* exponentially.

Proof.
The effective evaluation delay in the DDE becomes \tau_{\text{eff}}. The characteristic equation is then \lambda + \beta + \alpha e^{-\lambda\tau_{\text{eff}}} = 0. By Metatheorem 6.2, for \tau_{\text{eff}} > \tau_c there exists a root with \Re(\lambda) > 0. Standard DDE stability results imply that the full non‑linear system inherits this instability in a neighbourhood of the equilibrium. ∎

Remark. This result shows that timing, rather than content, can compromise an RSI loop, and it does not require the adversary to corrupt any semantic output of the validator.

9.3 Mitigation and Monitoring

Heuristic 9.1 (Latency Monitor L7).
The evaluation delay \tau is continuously measured. A reference distribution of \tau is constructed during a trusted pre‑deployment calibration phase. If the measured mean delay over a sliding window deviates from the reference mean by more than 3 standard deviations (empirically determined), the loop is halted and the validator queue is inspected for adversarial inputs. Additionally, a per‑evaluation hard timeout is enforced to cap the maximum delay from any single input.

This monitor is an engineering heuristic; its efficacy depends on the stationarity of the reference distribution and the detectability of the adversary’s injections at the 3\sigma level.

