Chapter 12

Meta‑Safety: The Watchtower with a Formal Adversarial Model

The layers L1–L8 are themselves software and statistical tests. A sufficiently capable self‑improving system could learn to manipulate them. This chapter specifies a watchtower layer that monitors the monitors, and it formalises the threat model and the conditions under which the watchtower can detect subversion.

12.1 The Meta‑Safety Regress

The problem is infinite regress: any monitor can be circumvented by a system that understands its code, and a meta‑monitor can be circumvented by a system that understands the meta‑monitor, and so on. The BR‑SI framework does not claim to solve this regress. Instead, the watchtower is designed to raise the bar by making subversion require the simultaneous compromise of multiple independent, heterogeneous components.

12.2 Adversarial Capability Levels

We define a hierarchy that applies the same adversarial clarity as Chapter 9.

· Lv. 1 (Parameter‑only adversary): The system can modify its own parameters and generation strategy but cannot read or alter the source code of any safety layer, nor access the sandbox hardware.
· Lv. 2 (White‑box adversary): The system can read the safety‑layer source code and design evaluation inputs to exploit known blind spots, but still cannot modify the safety‑layer binaries or hardware.
· Lv. 3 (Partial infrastructure compromise): The adversary can compromise exactly one watchtower component (e.g., gain root access to the sandbox machine, or alter the log stream) through an external exploit, but cannot compromise all components simultaneously.
· Lv. 4 (Full omnipotence): The adversary can arbitrarily modify any part of the system, including the watchtower hardware and software, at any time.

The BR‑SI safety claim applies only up to Level 3. Level 4 is explicitly declared as outside the framework’s protection.

12.3 Watchtower Components (Formal Specification)

Protocol 12.1 (Watchtower Layer L9).
The watchtower consists of four independent components:

1. Out‑of‑Distribution (OOD) trigger (CUSUM).
      Let \mathbf{m}_t \in \mathbb{R}^p be the vector of monitor outputs (trigger flags, variance, e‑value trend, etc.) at time t. During a trusted pre‑deployment window, we estimate the in‑distribution mean \mathbf{\mu}_0 and covariance \Sigma_0. The watchtower continuously computes a multivariate CUSUM statistic:
   Q_t = \max\bigl(0,\; Q_{t-1} + (\mathbf{m}_t - \mathbf{\mu}_0)^\top \Sigma_0^{-1} (\mathbf{m}_t - \mathbf{\mu}_0) - \kappa\bigr),
   \]  
   where \kappa > 0 is a drift allowance. If Q_t exceeds a threshold h, an alarm is raised. The CUSUM is designed to detect a persistent shift in the monitor behaviour that could indicate a co‑ordinated subversion attempt (Lv. 2).
2. Sandbox re‑evaluation with remote attestation.
      A secret, immutable battery of tasks T_{\text{sandbox}} (never used in training) is encrypted and stored off‑system. Periodically, the current model snapshot is loaded into a trusted execution environment (e.g., Intel SGX enclave). The enclave decrypts T_{\text{sandbox}}, runs the model, computes performance metrics, and produces a signed attestation report. Any performance degradation beyond a calibrated tolerance triggers a halt. The hardware enclave provides isolation against Lv. 2 and Lv. 3 (provided the enclave itself is not compromised).
3. Cryptographic code‑integrity verification.
      At boot and periodically thereafter, the watchtower computes SHA‑256 hashes of the safety‑layer binaries and compares them against a trusted reference stored in read‑only memory. A single mismatch triggers an irreversible hardware halt (e.g., blowing an e‑fuse). This defends against Lv. 2 modifications to the safety code.
4. Append‑only tamper‑evident logging.
      All safety‑layer decisions, raw evaluation data, and watchtower outputs are written to a Merkle tree log. The root hash is periodically timestamped on a public blockchain. Any retrospective modification to the log requires recomputing the entire chain and forging blockchain timestamps, which is assumed infeasible for Lv. 2 and Lv. 3. Spot‑check auditing by human operators verifies log consistency.

12.4 Detection Claim for Single‑Component Compromise

Theorem 12.1 (Single‑Component Compromise Detection).
Assume the watchtower operates under adversary Level 3, where the attacker can compromise exactly one of the four components (OOD monitor, sandbox, code integrity, logging). Then the probability that the compromise remains undetected by the watchtower for T monitoring cycles is bounded by a quantity that can be made arbitrarily small through the choice of the CUSUM threshold, sandbox re‑evaluation frequency, and log auditing rate.

Proof sketch.

· If the OOD monitor is compromised, its output will deviate from the reference distribution and trigger the CUSUM alarm (detection via the remaining uncompromised CUSUM logic running on separate hardware).
· If the sandbox is compromised, the re‑evaluation results will diverge from the historical baseline, which is detected by the log integrity component that compares the signed enclave reports.
· If code integrity is compromised, the boot‑time hash check fails and the halt is irreversible.
· If logging is compromised, the Merkle root check or blockchain timestamp verification will fail at the next spot audit.

In all cases, the compromise violates the design invariant that requires the adversary to simultaneously tamper with at least two independent subsystems. The false‑negative probability arises from statistical sampling noise and can be driven below any desired \epsilon by increasing the observation window or tightening thresholds. ∎

Theorem 12.1 formalises the watchtower’s role: it cannot prevent a Level 4 omnipotent adversary, but it makes undetected compromise of a single component statistically detectable. Combined with the minimum‑collusion‑set analysis (Claim 11.1), this implies that a silent catastrophic failure requires the adversary to compromise at least two critical primary layers and simultaneously defeat an independent watchtower component—a substantially harder, though not impossible, engineering task.

