Chapter 13

Conclusion: Detectable Failure as a Design Principle

This dissertation has argued a single, falsifiable thesis: a recursive self‑improvement loop can be structured such that, under a finite and transparent set of assumptions, every catastrophic failure mode either triggers a halt or is assigned a bounded probability of going undetected. We have not proved that RSI is safe; we have constructed a mathematical architecture that makes failure detectable—a property that may be achievable even when absolute safety is not.

The architecture is a compositional stack: a causal‑robustness filter (Chapter 4) catches the most obvious proxy‑gaming; an efficiency monitor (Chapter 5) flags optimisation pathologies; an SD‑DDE model (Chapter 6) exposes delay‑induced instability; a contamination protocol (Chapter 7) isolates evaluation data and identifies social contamination as an open problem; a Goodhart tail diagnostic (Chapter 8) catches heavy‑tailed proxy‑goal mismatches; a latency‑poisoning defence (Chapter 9) hardens the timing channel; a non‑i.i.d. SGM gate (Chapter 10) provides anytime‑valid statistical acceptance with a quantified circularity risk; and a watchtower (Chapter 12) audits the monitors themselves up to a explicit adversarial capability level.

The compositional analysis (Chapter 11) demonstrates that the layers do not conflict and that the joint false‑positive budget is controlled. The Honesty Principle, far from undermining the framework, is its foundation: by refusing to claim global causal certification, we force every other layer to be built for monitoring and detection, not for unreachable guarantees.

Every theorem, proposition, and heuristic has been labelled with its logical status and its domain of validity. The degradation pathways when assumptions break are described. The watchtower acknowledges its own incompleteness in the face of a fully omnipotent adversary. The framework is thus offered not as a final answer, but as a falsifiable blueprint: a structure that can be implemented, attacked, and refined. A theory that knows where it breaks is the only theory that can be trusted in the presence of recursive self‑modification—and it is the only one that can be improved.

