Appendix A: Mathematical Background and Derivations

A.1 Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics and the Fokker‑Planck Equation

We consider a loss function L: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R} and the SGLD update

\theta_{k+1} = \theta_k - \eta \nabla \tilde{L}(\theta_k) + \sqrt{2\eta\beta^{-1}}\,\xi_k, \qquad \xi_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I).
\]  

In the continuous‑time limit \eta \to 0, the process \theta_t converges weakly to the solution of the stochastic differential equation

d\theta_t = -\nabla L(\theta_t)\,dt + \sqrt{2\beta^{-1}}\,dW_t,
\]  

where W_t is a standard d‑dimensional Brownian motion. The probability density p(\theta,t) evolves according to

\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (p\,\nabla L) + \beta^{-1}\Delta p.
\]  

Under mild growth conditions on L (e.g., L is C^2 and \|\nabla L(\theta)\| \to \infty as \|\theta\| \to \infty sufficiently fast) the stationary distribution is the Gibbs measure

p_*(\theta) = \frac{1}{Z} \exp(-\beta L(\theta)), \qquad Z = \int e^{-\beta L(\theta)}\,d\theta.
\]  

A quadratic approximation around a local minimum \theta^* yields a Gaussian stationary distribution with covariance \beta^{-1}H^{-1}, where H = \nabla^2 L(\theta^*). The trace of H can be related to the layer‑wise weight variance T(t) defined in the main text. The efficiency–variance inequality (Theorem 5.1) follows from a Taylor expansion and taking expectations; the full derivation is provided in Appendix B.

A.2 State‑Dependent Delay‑Differential Equations

For a function f depending on the delayed state C(t-\tau(C(t))), linearisation begins by finding an equilibrium C^* such that f(C^*, E^*, I/O^*) = 0. Let x(t) = C(t) - C^*. Expanding to first order gives

\dot{x}(t) \approx \frac{\partial f}{\partial C_{\text{delayed}}} x(t-\tau(C^*)) + \frac{\partial f}{\partial C_{\text{instantaneous}}} x(t) + \frac{\partial f}{\partial \tau}\tau'(C^*) x(t).
\]  

Combining terms yields the linear DDE

\dot{x}(t) = -\alpha x(t-\tau^*) - \beta x(t),
\]  

where \alpha = -\frac{\partial f}{\partial C_{\text{delayed}}} > 0 for stability, \beta includes both instantaneous feedback and the delay‑gradient contribution. The characteristic equation is derived by substituting x(t) = e^{\lambda t}, leading to \lambda + \beta + \alpha e^{-\lambda\tau^*} = 0. The analysis of critical delays uses the D‑subdivision method: setting \lambda = i\omega and solving for real and imaginary parts gives parametric equations for stability boundaries.

A.3 E‑values and Anytime‑Valid Inference

Let H_0 be a null hypothesis. A non‑negative random variable E is an e‑variable if \mathbb{E}_{H_0}[E] \le 1. Under a filtration \{\mathcal{F}_t\}, a sequence E_1, E_2, \dots such that each E_t is \mathcal{F}_t-measurable and \mathbb{E}[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] \le 1 under H_0 defines a test super‑martingale W_t = \prod_{i=1}^t E_i. Ville’s inequality states \mathbb{P}(\sup_t W_t \ge 1/\alpha) \le \alpha for any \alpha \in (0,1). This yields anytime‑valid type‑I error control. The choice of e‑value for Gaussian observations with unknown variance borrows from the fact that \exp(\lambda Z - \lambda^2/2) is an e‑variable when Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1); for non‑i.i.d. sequences, the predictable plug‑in method replaces the fixed parameters by forecasts.

A.4 Hill Estimator for the Tail Index

For an i.i.d. sample from a distribution with a regularly varying tail of index \xi = 1/\alpha > 0, the Hill estimator is consistent under appropriate conditions on k. The asymptotic variance is \xi^2/k. Bootstrap stability procedures are used to select k in practice; see the algorithm in Appendix C.3.

