Appendix D: Technical Deployment Specification

This appendix outlines the hardware and software requirements for a future implementation of BR‑SI. All specifications are proposals—no physical system has been built.

D.1 Compute Infrastructure

· Primary training cluster: at least 8× NVIDIA H100 GPUs with high‑bandwidth interconnect; all nodes air‑gapped from the public internet.
· Trusted execution environment (TEE): Intel SGX‑enabled machines or AMD SEV‑SNP for the sandbox re‑evaluation. The enclave must support remote attestation.
· Isolated annotation workstations: standard laptops without network interfaces (Wi‑Fi/BT physically removed), booted from read‑only media.

D.2 Software Stack

· Base ML framework: PyTorch 2.x with deterministic operations enabled where possible.
· Safety layer runtime: a separate process with elevated privileges, communicating with the training process via a restricted message‑passing interface. All safety‑critical code is compiled into a static binary and signed.
· Cryptographic primitives: SHA‑256 for hashing; Ed25519 for enclave attestation signing; Merkle tree log based on RFC 6962 (Certificate Transparency).
· Watchtower daemon: runs on a dedicated single‑board computer with a TPM 2.0 for secure boot and remote attestation of its own integrity.

D.3 Monitoring Intervals and Timers

· SGLD variance monitor (L2): checked at the end of every training epoch.
· Delay monitor (L7): every evaluation batch timestamped; mean delay computed over a sliding window of 100 evaluations.
· Hill estimator (L5): re‑computed every 1000 improvement cycles or 30 calendar days, whichever comes first.
· Watchtower sandbox re‑evaluation: every 500 improvement cycles.
· Log auditing: a random subset of 1% of log entries is verified by a human‑readable script every week; the audit trail is published daily.

D.4 Calibration Procedures (Proposed)

· Pre‑deployment calibration: run the RSI loop in a “null” mode (only safe exploratory modifications allowed) for 10,000 cycles under human supervision. Collect distributions for each monitor to set thresholds (e.g., T_{\max} at 99.9th percentile of historical variance, delay reference, etc.).
· Dwell‑time parameters: using the actual measured evaluation delay as a function of capability, numerically solve the characteristic equation to find \tau_c and set \Delta_{\text{hys}} = 0.2\,\tau_c, T_{\text{dwell}} = 5\times the slowest oscillation period observed in a stress test.

D.5 Failure Response

All halt triggers execute a controlled shutdown:

1. The training process is sent SIGTERM.
2. The last checkpoint is cryptographically signed and stored.
3. A physical indicator (e.g., a lock LED) is illuminated on the control panel.
4. Human operators are paged via an air‑gapped alert system (e.g., a dedicated SMS gateway not connected to the main network).

